do not discard F2 or F3**\n\nW8\u00d72: good pair base. Keep.\n\nW9: singleton, part of high end. Could help 7-8-9, but not urgent.\n\nSo among safest and most strategic:\n\n\u2192 **Discard T9**: removes a stray suit tile, safe, moves toward Half Flush.\n\nAlternatively, **discard T2** \u2014 equally valid.\n\nBut **T9 is a terminal**, less flexible, and more likely to be dead weight.\n\nAlso, in terms of future waits: holding T2 allows possibility of chow with T3/T4 \u2014 but I already have an exposed meld, and drawing T3/T4 is unlikely to help.\n\nNo benefit.\n\nHence, **Play T9** is best.\n\nBut let's check valid actions: [Play T9; Play W5; Play W9; Play W2; Play W8; Play W6; Play F3; Play T2; Play F2]\n\nWe want to avoid W5/W6 due to [W5,W6,W7] exposure.\n\nAvoid W2? W2 is safe \u2014 W1 and W3 are scarce, so [W1,W2,W3] unlikely.\n\nBut still, better not to risk.\n\nF3: seat wind, but only one \u2014 hard to use. Can discard, but F2\u00d72 is more valuable.\n\nSo between T9 and T2, both safe.\n\nBut **I already discarded T9 before** (in history: Play T9), and no one claimed \u2014 confirms it\u2019s safe.\n\nSimilarly, I played T4 earlier \u2014 no claim.\n\nSo **T9 is confirmed safe**.\n\nFinal choice: **Play T9**\n\nReason: Removes a non-Character tile, aiding potential Half Flush development; T9 is a terminal and less useful; and it's confirmed safe by prior discard history.\n\nAnswer: Play T9"}
