to win is to draw W7 and make [W7W8W9].\n\nIf I discard W8, I can win by drawing W9 and ponging, or by self-drawing W9 and declaring kong or just adding, but usually you need to have two to pong.\n\nWith two W9s in hand, if I draw the third, I can declare a concealed kong or just keep.\n\nBut to win, I need to declare the pung.\n\nSo yes.\n\nSo discarding W8 allows me to keep two W9s, so I can pong if W9 is discarded, or if I draw W9, I can complete the pung.\n\nWhereas discarding W9 leaves me with only one W9, so I cannot make pung.\n\nSo better to discard W8.\n\nThen I have two W9s.\n\nAnd I can still try to make [W7W8W9]? No \u2014 I discarded W8.\n\nSo I cannot.\n\nSo only hope is W9 pung.\n\nBut I also have the F3 pung.\n\nAnd the two chows.\n\nSo same structure.\n\nBut is there a difference in safety?\n\nDiscarding W8: W8 unshown 3 \u2014 not dangerous.\n\nW9: unshown 3 \u2014 similar.\n\nBut W8 is in a chow with previous player \u2014 he has [W5W6W7] \u2014 might be waiting for W8? He discarded W7 \u2014 probably not.\n\nSimilarly, W9 \u2014 no one seems to need.\n\nBut by discarding W8, I keep the option for W9 pung.\n\nMoreover, W9 is terminal \u2014 pung of terminal \u2014 1 point.\n\nF3 is wind \u2014 also 1 point.\n\nSame.\n\nBut if I make W9 pung, I have two terminal/honor pungs \u2014 \"Pung of Terminals or Honors\" can be counted twice? Yes \u2014 the pattern can be counted more than once.\n\nSo if I have F3 pung and W9 pung, that's 2 points.\n\nPreviously, only 1.\n\nThen: 2 (Pung x2) + 1 (One Voided Suit) + 1 (Self-Drawn) = 5 \u2014 still not 8.\n\nNeed more.\n\nPerhaps \"Last Tile Claim\" or something, but not controllable.\n\nAnother pattern: \"Chicken Hand\" \u2014 8 points \u2014 if no other patterns.\n\nBut I have some.\n\n\"Chicken Hand\" is for hands with zero fans, but here I have at least 1.\n\nSo not.\n\nPerhaps I missed a pattern.\n\n\"Half Flush\"? No \u2014 two suits.\n\n\"Outside Hand\"? Let's see: each meld must contain terminals or honors.\n\n- [T5T6T7]: 5,6,7 \u2014 not terminals \u2014 so no.\n\n- [W6W7W8]: 6,7,8 \u2014 not terminals.\n\nSo not.\n\n\"All Simples\"? No \u2014 I have honors.\n\nSo scoring is low.\n\nBut perhaps I can go for \"Big Three Winds\" \u2014 but I have F3, F2, need F1.\n\nI have no F1.\n\nAnd I have only two F2s.\n\nSo hard.\n\nPerhaps the best is to discard W9, and aim for [W7W8W9] to get \"Short Straight\" for 2 points.\n\nBut still not enough.\n\nAnother idea: discard F2.\n\nThen I have only one F2 \u2014 cannot make pair.\n\nSo not.\n\nOr discard F3 \u2014 then I have two F3s \u2014 can make pair, but lose the pung.\n\nThen no \"Pung of Terminals or Honors\".\n\nWorse.\n\nSo the only reasonable choices are to discard W8 or W9.\n\nBetween them, discarding W8 allows me to keep two W9s, so I can potentially make a W9 pung, which gives an additional point if I count \"Pung of Terminals or Honors\" twice.\n\nBut I still need more points.\n\nPerhaps \"Concealed Hand\" \u2014 but I have exposed melds.\n\nNo.\n\nMaybe I can win by \"Robbing the Kong\" or other, but not now.\n\nPerhaps the answer is to discard W9, as it is less useful.\n\nBut let's look at the valid actions: Play W9; Play F3; Play F2; Play W8.\n\nAnd I must choose one.\n\nPerhaps the safest is to discard W9, as it is duplicated, and I have the F3 pung to fall back on.\n\nBut I think discarding W8 is better because it allows me to keep the W9 pair option.\n\nBut I can't have both.\n\nUltimately, the hand is weak, and I need to minimize risk.\n\nAnother factor: defense.\n\nWho might be close to winning?\n\nNext player has [B7B8B9], [W3W3W3] \u2014 so he has a terminal chow and a terminal pung.\n\nHe might be going for something.\n\nAcross has [B7B8B9], [T1T2T3] \u2014 two terminal chows.\n\nAlso dangerous.\n\nPrevious has [W5W6W7] \u2014 middle.\n\nSo next and across might be close.\n\nThey might need W8 or W9.\n\nW8: previous has W7, might want W8 for [W7W8W9].\n\nBut he has [W5W6W7], so might be going for [W7W8W9].\n\nSimilarly, across has no W.\n\nNext has [W3W3W3], so might not need W8.\n\nBut could be.\n\nTo be safe, don't discard W8 or W9.\n\nBut I have to.\n\nF2 and F3 are safer.\n\nF2: no one has discarded, so might be safe.\n\nF3: I discarded one, and no one has claimed, so probably safe.\n\nBut I have three F3s \u2014 if I discard one, I break the pung.\n\nSo best to discard W9.\n\nIt's a terminal, and I have two, so one extra.\n\nSo I'll go with that.\n\nFinal decision: discard W9.\n\nSo I can still aim for [W7W8W9] if I draw W7.\n\nAnd keep the F3 pung.\n\nReason: To maintain the concealed pung of F3 for scoring and to enable the possibility of forming the [W7,W8,W9] chow for the \"Short Straight\" pattern, while discarding a redundant terminal tile.\n\nAnswer: Play W9"}
