iscard. And if my pungs are formed by Peng (not yet), then they are also exposed. But currently, my F3 pung is **still concealed** unless I Peng.\n\nAh! Important: The hand shows I have **[F3, F3, F3]** \u2014 that's a **concealed pung**, since no Peng action was declared in history. Similarly, F2s are just two \u2014 not a pung yet.\n\nSo currently:\n- Melds via Chi: exposed\n- F3\u00d73: concealed pung\n- Others: singles\n\nSo total: 2 exposed melds (chows), 1 concealed pung (F3), plus loose tiles.\n\nTo win, I need one more meld and a pair.\n\nBest direction: Aim for **All Pungs** (6 points) by turning F2 into a pung (via draw or Peng) and find one more pung/kong. Or go for **Half Flush** (6 points): mostly W and honors \u2014 very feasible.\n\nMy hand is almost entirely Winds and Characters \u2014 perfect for **Half Flush** (honors + one suit). Currently, I have:\n- Suit: W1, W8, W8 (and W6, W7, W8 already melded)\n- Honors: F2\u00d72, F3\u00d73\n\nSo after melding, hand is rich in W and F. Only T5-T7 is off-suit \u2014 but that's already a meld.\n\nSo **Half Flush** is achievable \u2014 6 points.\n\nAdditionally:\n- *Pung of F3 (Honors)*: 2 points\n- *Prevalent Wind (F2)*: if I make F2 pung \u2192 2 points\n- *Two Dragon Pungs*: not applicable\n- *Mixed Double Pung*: if I make both F2 and F3 pungs \u2192 same number? No, different winds. But both are *Pung of Terminals or Honors* \u2192 each counts separately \u2192 2\u00d72 = 4 points? No: the pattern says \u201ccan be counted more than once\u201d, but only if multiple such melds exist.\n\nFrom scoring rules:\n> Pung of Terminals or Honors: A hand with a pung or kong of terminal tiles or honor tiles. This pattern can be counted more than once if there are multiple melds meeting this requirement.\n\nYes! So if I have **two pungs of honors**, I get 2 points \u00d7 2 = **4 points**.\n\nCurrently, F3 pung = 1 such meld \u2192 2 points.\n\nIf I make F2 pung \u2192 second such meld \u2192 additional 2 points \u2192 total 4 points.\n\nAlso, **Prevalent Wind (F2)** adds **2 points**.\n\nAnd **Half Flush** adds **6 points**.\n\nPlus, possibly **All Pungs** (6 points) if I can convert W1+W8\u00d72 into a pung or pair.\n\nWait: W1 and W8 are different numbers. Can\u2019t make pung unless I draw more W1 or W8.\n\nBut I have W1\u00d71, W8\u00d72. If I draw another W8, I can make pung of W8, or if I draw W1, still only two.\n\nBetter: aim for pair of W8 or F2.\n\nBest target: Complete **F2 pung** and keep **F3 pung**, have **pair of W8**, and treat T5-T7 and W6-W7-W8 as two chows \u2014 but wait, I already have **two chows**, so I can't have four pungs.\n\nWait \u2014 contradiction: I have **two chows already formed via Chi** \u2014 so I cannot claim All Pungs (which requires four pungs/kongs). So **All Pungs** is impossible.\n\nSimilarly, **All Chows** is broken by the two pungs I\u2019m forming.\n\nSo best viable patterns:\n- **Half Flush** (6 points): Yes \u2014 one suit (Characters) + honors\n- **Pung of Terminals or Honors** \u00d72 (4 points): F3 pung + F2 pung\n- **Prevalent Wind** (2 points): F2 pung\n\u2192 Total: 6+4+2 = **12 points** \u2014 sufficient to win.\n\nAlternatively, if I don\u2019t complete F2 pung, I lose 4 points (2 for extra Pung of Honors, 2 for Prevalent Wind).\n\nNow, back to valid actions:\n\n- **Play F3**: Breaks my concealed pung. Leaves F3\u00d72. Risky \u2014 I lose guaranteed 2 points immediately. If someone discards F3 later, I could Peng, but then it's exposed \u2014 still counts for pattern, but I lose chance to keep concealed. But main issue: I'm voluntarily giving up a scoring element. Unwise.\n- **Play F2**: Breaks my F2 pair. Leaves only one F2. Kills chance of completing F2 pung unless I draw it. With 4 F2 unshown (many!), possible, but risky. Also, F2 is **prevailing wind** \u2014 dangerous to discard early, but others may avoid it too. Still, sacrificing opportunity is bad.\n- **Play W8**: I have two W8s. One is already used in [W6,W7,W8] meld? Wait \u2014 no: when you form a meld via Chi, you add the discarded tile to your hand to complete the sequence. So the [W6,W7,W8] meld uses one W8 from my hand. So I had at least one W8 in hand before Chi. Now I have one W8 left? Or two?\n\nHand is listed as: [W1:1, W8:2, F2:2, F3:3]\n\nSo **after** forming [W6,W7,W8] via Chi, I still have **two W8s** \u2014 meaning I originally had three W8s? Or did I draw after?\n\nPossible: I had two W8s initially, used one to complete the chow via Chi (so I claimed W7 or W6?), no: Chi means I used two from hand and one from discard.\n\nLet\u2019s clarify: To perform 'Chi W7', I must have had W6 and W8 in hand, and claimed W7 from discard to form [W6,W7,W8].\n\nBut in that case, I used one W6 and one W8 from hand.\n\nBut I don\u2019t have W6 in current hand \u2014 makes sense.\n\nBut I still have **W8:2** \u2014 so I must have had three W8s originally, used one in the chow, still have two left.\n\nYes \u2014 possible. So I have two W8s in hand now.\n\nSo playing W8 would leave one W8 \u2014 breaks the pair, but W8 is not terminal or dragon \u2014 no special value.\n\nBut I could use W8 pair as eye (pair) for winning hand.\n\nAlternatively, if I draw an eighth W8? No, only four copies.\n\nW8: unshown=3, so three left in wall/discarded. But I already used one in meld, have two \u2014 so three accounted for, one possibly discarded.\n\nAnyway, W8 pair is useful as **eye**.\n\nBut F2 pair is more valuable \u2014 because completing pung gives 4 extra points (vs 0 for W8 pung).\n\nSo better to preserve F2\u00d72.\n\nF3\u00d73 is already a pung \u2014 safest to keep all three.\n\nW1 is singleton \u2014 no pair, no sequence.\n\nSo best discard is **W1** \u2014 it contributes nothing to patterns, not helping any chow or pung.\n\nWhereas:\n- F3\u00d73: core scoring element\n- F2\u00d72: foundation for high-value pung\n- W8\u00d72: potential pair/eye\n\nThus, **Play W1** is the safest and most logical discard.\n\nIt doesn\u2019t damage any scoring potential, keeps flexibility.\n\nReason: Preserving the concealed pung of F3 (West Wind) and the pair of F2 (South Wind, prevalent) maximizes scoring potential. Completing a pung of F2 would yield both *Pung of Terminals or Honors* (2 points) and *Prevalent Wind* (2 points), while retaining the existing F3 pung gives another 2 points. The W1 tile is a deadwood tile with no immediate use in forming valuable melds or supporting a flush. Discarding it maintains the integrity of multiple scoring patterns.\n\nAnswer: Play W1"}
